{# DECIDED: alertmanager-callers sits in the observability namespace and admits loki-homelab
   from the homelab namespace, so it stays bespoke.
   The callers model (`netpol_from` / `netpol_fences`, macro in
   ansible/templates/networkpolicy.yml.j2) renders one TCP port admitting same-namespace `app:`
   peers, and its header rules out growing it to absorb another shape. Census: #3701.
#}
---
# Alertmanager has one cross-namespace, in-cluster caller: loki-homelab's ruler, which sends
# each firing LogQL alert to `alertmanager_url` (loki-homelab/templates/configmap.yaml.j2). The
# M5 plan (docs/target-architecture/monitoring/m5-loki-ruler.md, "The cross-namespace grant")
# lands this grant after the ruler, so the ruler's refused sends were recorded first.
#
# The loki-homelab pod runs one container and no sidecar, so `app: loki-homelab` admits the
# ruler and nothing else. Alloy, the other workload in that role, is `app: alloy` and is not
# admitted.
#
# Every other caller is already admitted by networkpolicy-observability.yaml.j2, which still
# selects alertmanager; NetworkPolicies add up, so this file only widens it:
#   - prometheus, its only alert source before the ruler, and its own scrape of the
#     `alertmanager` job: the intra-namespace `podSelector: {}`.
#   - Alertmanager has no route, so traefik never dials it.
#
# 9093 is the containerPort AND the Service targetPort (observability/templates/alertmanager.yaml.j2).
#
# INGRESS ONLY, same as the rest of this role: an Egress policy selects pods correctly on this
# cluster and blocks nothing (measured 2026-08-07). Writing one would read like a control in the
# repo and do nothing in the cluster.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: alertmanager-callers
  namespace: {{ k8s_observability_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: alertmanager
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# `| bool`, matching the `when:` in tasks/main.yml, for the string trap
   networkpolicy-prometheus.yaml.j2 explains in full. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_obs_enforced | bool %}
    - from:
        # namespaceSelector and podSelector are SIBLING KEYS in ONE list item, so they AND
        # together: loki-homelab, but only inside {{ k8s_namespace }}. As two separate list
        # items they would OR, admitting the entire {{ k8s_namespace }} namespace, which the
        # probe's refused alertmanager row would then reach.
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: {{ k8s_namespace }}
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: loki-homelab
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 9093
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
